  "summary": "Fin-headed rocketships and zap-guns — the future drawn in bright pulp ink.",
  "description": "Raygun Gothic is the term (coined by William Gibson in \"The Gernsback Continuum\") for the pulp-SF visual language of 1926-1950s: swooping finned rocketships, bubble canopies, needle-nosed zap guns, dials-and-bubbles control panels and underdome cities — the future as Amazing Stories cover art. Its designers borrowed from streamliners, deco and world's-fair futurism; its legacy runs through Forbidden Planet props, Tomorrowland, The Iron Giant, and every toy rocket that means \"space age\". The style is optimistic, drawn-curve smooth, and permanently associated with chrome and starlight.",
  "culturalContext": "Raygun Gothic is the pulp future's afterlife: named by William Gibson, the style's finned rockets and chrome optimism shaped Forbidden Planet, Tomorrowland and toy culture, and it remains the visual shorthand for \"space age\" whenever design wants innocent confidence.",
